<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The ground CALVIN's play table stands on, and a sky over it.

  MetaSim loads this as `ScenarioCfg.scene`, which makes it the *root* of the
  MJCF the MuJoCo backend assembles: the table, the robot and the blocks are
  attached into it. So it owns what is true of the whole scene rather than of
  any one object.

  Pass `add_default_ground=False` alongside it. MetaSim's own floor is a grey
  checker plane at z=0 and would land in exactly the same place as this one,
  z-fighting with it.

  CALVIN itself has no scene beyond the floor: `calvin_env` loads
  `plane/plane.urdf` and leaves the rest of the frame to whatever the viewport
  clears to, which is black. That is honest for a policy which only ever sees
  two camera feeds cropped to the bench, and useless for looking at, which is
  what these examples are for. So the floor here is CALVIN's, measured off its
  own asset, and the sky is this file's addition.

  Walls were tried and taken out again. From 8 m a 2.6 m wall covers the top of
  a frame from most angles and not from the corners, so an orbit pans a bright
  wedge of sky in and out; taller walls just put flat grey where the sky is.
  Floor meeting sky has a horizon and no seams.

  No lights here, on purpose. They come from `ScenarioCfg.lights` (see
  `tasks/rakuda_calvin.py`), which is the one lighting description both the
  MuJoCo and the Isaac Sim backends read.
-->
<mujoco model="calvin_room">
  <asset>
    <!--
      A sky, so the upper half of every frame is not #000000. Gradient rather
      than an HDRI: MuJoCo's skybox is not image-based lighting, it only fills
      the background, so a 12 KB procedural gradient buys exactly as much as a
      30 MB .hdr would. The Isaac Sim backend gets the real thing instead, from
      `RenderCfg.hdri_path` / `DomeLightCfg.texture_file`.
    -->
    <texture name="skybox" type="skybox" builtin="gradient"
             rgb1="0.55 0.64 0.74" rgb2="0.84 0.88 0.92" width="256" height="1536"/>

    <!-- CALVIN's floor, unmodified. -->
    <texture name="calvin_floor" type="2d" file="textures/checker_blue.png"/>
    <!--
      Both numbers are measured off `plane/plane.obj` and `plane.mtl` rather
      than picked, so the floor comes out the size and the shade calvin_env
      renders it:

        texrepeat  plane.obj is 30 m across with UVs running 0..15, so the
                   image tiles once every 2 m; with texuniform the unit of
                   texrepeat is repeats per metre, hence 0.5. The image is a
                   4x4 checker, so a square is 0.5 m, which is what a CALVIN
                   frame shows.
        rgba       `Kd 0.588` in plane.mtl. The image itself is white on very
                   light grey; without that factor the floor blows out to a
                   white sheet, which is what the first attempt here did.
    -->
    <material name="calvin_floor" texture="calvin_floor" texrepeat="0.5 0.5" texuniform="true"
              rgba="0.588 0.588 0.588 1" specular="0.1" shininess="0.1" reflectance="0.03"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!--
      30 m, the size of CALVIN's own plane. The horizon then sits far enough
      out that an orbiting camera never reaches the edge of it.
    -->
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="15 15 0.05" pos="0 0 0" material="calvin_floor"
          condim="3" conaffinity="15" friction="1 0.005 0.0001"/>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
